p.src:
Compare two simple approaches. Along the $x$-direction, $dy=0$, so $\left.\frac{dw}{dz}\right|_x
  =\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}
  +i\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}.$ Along the $y$-direction, $dx=0$, and $\left.\frac{dw}{dz}\right|_y
  =\frac{1}{i}\frac{\partial u}{\partial y}
  +\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}
  =\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}
  -i\frac{\partial u}{\partial y}.$ Equality of real and imaginary parts gives $\boxed{
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}
  =\frac{\partial v}{\partial y},
  \qquad
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}
  =-\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}}$ These are the Cauchy–Riemann equations. Under the usual differentiability assumptions they are also sufficient for the limit  to be direction independent. A function with this property is analytic, or holomorphic, at the point.

