p.src:
Write two small complex displacements in polar form: $\delta z=\rho e^{i\theta},
  \qquad
  \Delta z=\rho'e^{i\theta'}.$ Their ratio has phase $\theta-\theta'$, exactly the angle between them. Under an analytic map, $\frac{\delta w}{\delta z}
  =\frac{\Delta w}{\Delta z}
  =w'(z),$ because the derivative does not depend on direction. Rearranging gives $\frac{\Delta z}{\delta z}
  =\frac{\Delta w}{\delta w}.$ The two sides have the same phase, so the angle between the original intervals equals the angle between their images.

